<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Onfim</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Onfim was a boy who lived in Novgorod in the thirteenth century, probably aged six or seven, known from a group of birchbark documents found by archaeologists in 1956. They contain his writing exercises, copied prayers and drawings of himself as a warrior on horseback slaying enemies. They are a rare record of childhood and schooling in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Onfim lived in Novgorod, which at the time was the capital of the Novgorod Republic, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> that had formally become independent in the 12th century. The city is surrounded by birch forests, and for centuries the local people wrote on the bark, which was soft and easily scratched. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> the city was excavated systematically, and since 1951 more than 1,100 pieces of birch bark bearing writing have been found. Onfim's pieces (nos. 199 to 208 and 210) came from the Nerevsky excavation site in 1956, and a further letter (no. 331) was later found in an adjacent area.</p><p>Scholars believe that the Novgorod Republic had an unusually high level of literacy for its time, spread across social classes and among both sexes. One estimate puts 20 per cent of the urban male population of Russian city-states as literate around the middle of the 13th century. Before the birch bark finds, most scholars had assumed that medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> was almost entirely illiterate and that only the wealthy and the clergy could read. The study of the documents is called berestology, from the Russian word for birch bark.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Seventeen birch bark items by Onfim are known, dated to the mid-13th century. Twelve carry drawings and five only text. They are plainly learning exercises: he wrote out the alphabet, repeated syllables and copied psalms that were presumably familiar to him, including fragments of Psalms 6:2 and 27:3 and the phrase &quot;Lord, help your servant Onfim&quot;. Most of his writing consists of citations from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-psalms">Book of Psalms</a>.</p><p>His drawings show knights, horses, arrows and slain enemies. On one a knight on horseback, with Onfim's name beside him, stabs a man on the ground with a lance, and scholars suggest the boy pictured himself as the knight. Item no. 199, once the bottom of a birch bark basket, shows a long-necked beast with pointed ears and a curly tail; one text beside it reads &quot;I am a wild beast&quot;, and another &quot;Greetings from Onfim to Danilo&quot;, probably a friend or classmate. The people he drew have anywhere from three to eight fingers on each hand.</p><p>On item no. 206 four letters with a titlo above them were once read as a number, the year 1263, which fitted the age of the bark. That reading was debated for decades. A newer and more plausible interpretation takes the letters as the opening words of the six-o'clock troparion, so that Onfim was again practising by copying liturgical texts. He did not use the letters known as yers, writing О and Е in their place.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Onfim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Archaeologists have found over a thousand birchbark letters in Novgorod, including a thirteenth-century boy <b>Onfim</b>’s school exercises and drawings.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliky-novgorod">Veliky Novgorod</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=58.52,31.28&amp;year=1264" aria-label="Veliky Novgorod on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Onfim"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5057 5126h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5057 5126h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=58.52,31.28&amp;year=1264">What was here in 1264? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
